    field: 'Mathematics',
    knownFor: 'Differential equations and the spinning top',
    bio: 'Kovalevskaya was born in Moscow and earned a doctorate from Göttingen University in 1874. She taught in Stockholm from 1884, and in 1889 became the first woman in modern Europe to hold a full university chair. She won the Prix Bordin for work on how a solid body spins.',
    idea: 'Differential equations describe how things change. She proved when some of them are sure to have a solution, and used them to describe a spinning top.',
    rarity: 'rare',
    perk:'chapter',
    sources: ['https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Kovalevskaya/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sofya_Kovalevskaya', 'https://mathwomen.agnesscott.org/women/kova.htm'],
